1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,900
The record says: payment failed. What actually happened: the customer’s account was debited.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:09,300
Saturday 20 June, 6:48 pm. Store 014’s tills give up on UPI payments, read the way a data team reads them.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:15,200
The payments dashboard: approvals fall to 69% and 67% for two hours, then recover. Incident closed.

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:21,600
All 10 data tests pass. Every bill has exactly one approved payment.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:28,000
Reconciliation parks 26 UPI credits with no approved payment in suspense, until someone claims them.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,400
Match each timeout to the provider’s report by amount and time: 26 extra charges on 24 customers, and no false alarms.

7
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:42,300
An AI assistant with a domain layer: 7 of 7 answers right, not 1. Yes, 24 customers paid twice.

8
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:49,200
What actually happened: her bank had taken the money. The credit came 87 seconds later. She paid twice, and waited 8 days.

9
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:55,600
Payment success in those 90 minutes: 61% at the till, 82% at the bank, 100% of bills paid. 87% charged once.

10
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:02,000
Put the bill ID on every UPI request: each double charge is refunded the next morning. Median wait: 4.9 days → 0.7.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Every record was right about what it saw. Only the customer saw both debits.
